, return_inverse=True)\n    # v = np.bincount(inverse, weights=w, minlength=u.size)\n    v = np.bincount(inverse, weights=w, minlength=u.size).astype(np.float64)\n    # Due to possible zero? If w positive, v positive. But if underflow? w positive. Could have v=0 if weight underflow? w = t/Z, t positive subnormal maybe, Z maybe 1, w subnormal positive. bincount sum positive. okay.\n    m = u.size\n    if m == 1:\n        val = float(u[0])\n        return [val] * q_arr.size\n    C = np.cumsum(v, dtype=np.float64)\n    p = C - 0.5 * v\n    p1 = float(p[0]); pm = float(p[-1])\n    denom = pm - p1\n    # denom should >0. But due to rounding maybe 0? If m>=2 and v positive, denom >=? Could be very small if v1+vm close 1? Actually denom = 1 - (v1+vm)/2. If v1+vm close 2? But v1+vm <=1, so denom >=0.5. Wait v sum=1, v1+vm <=1, denom >=0.5. So not small. Good.\n    h = (p - p1) / denom\n    h[0] = 0.0; h[-1] = 1.0\n    # Ensure h increasing? Could have tiny non-monotonic due to rounding? p increasing, denom positive. But enforce? Maybe not.\n    # Vectorized query\n    res = np.empty(q_arr.size, dtype=np.float64)\n    low = q_arr <= 0.0\n    high = q_arr >= 1.0\n    mid = ~(low | high)\n    if np.any(low): res[low] = u[0]\n    if np.any(high): res[high] = u[-1]\n    if np.any(mid):\n       qm = q_arr[mid]\n       k = np.searchsorted(h, qm, side='right') - 1\n       # Due to q in (0,1), k should be 0..m-2. But if q is extremely close to 1 and h[-1]=1, searchsorted returns m-1? Let's verify: searchsorted([0,1], 0.9999999999999999, side='right') returns 1 (insert before index 1? Actually array length 2, values [0,1]. For x=0.999, insertion index 1 (before 1). side right same. k=0. Good. For x=1.0, high handled. For x=0.0, low handled. For x=nextafter(0,1), insertion index 1, k=0.\n       # But if h has duplicate due to rounding? Shouldn't. If k == m-1 (q >= h[-1]) but q<1? impossible if h[-1]=1. If q is 1 - 1e-324? float min subnormal, h[-1]=1, insertion index m-1? k=m-2. Good.\n       # If q is negative zero? low.\n       k = np.clip(k, 0, m-2)\n       denom_k = h[k+1] - h[k]\n       # If denom_k == 0 due to duplicate h? Could happen if v_k extremely small? But h strictly increasing mathematically. In float, if v_k underflows? v positive maybe subnormal, p difference maybe 0 if v_k/2 below precision relative to C? But denom overall >=0.5, h differences could be tiny and round to 0 if v_k extremely tiny relative to 1? Example v1=1-1e-300, v2=1e-300. p1=(1-1e-300)/2 ~0.5, p2=1 - 0.5e-300 ~1. h diff ~0.5. Not tiny. If middle v tiny, h diff tiny maybe 0. If h[k+1]==h[k], interpolation undefined. But mathematically interval weight tiny. Could handle by returning u[k] if denom_k==0. But tests unlikely. To be safe:\n       valid = denom_k != 0.0\n       # For invalid, result = u[k] (or u[k+1]? If h same, q equals both? Return u[k] maybe).\n       res_mid = np.empty_like(qm)\n       res_mid[valid] = u[k[valid]] + (qm[valid] - h[k[valid]]) * (u[k[valid]+1] - u[k[valid]]) / denom_k[valid]\n       if any invalid: res_mid[~valid] = u[k[~valid]]\n       res[mid] = res_mid\n    return [float(x) for x in res]\n\nNeed consider q_arr contains values just outside [0,1] due to float? Validation raises if <0 or >1. Good.\n\nPotential issue: np.unique on vals after mask2. If vals contain -0.0 and 0.0, unique returns one. Good.\nPotential issue: np.bincount inverse length. inverse dtype intp. Good.\nPotential issue: weights w sum to 1 but due to rounding v sum maybe 0.9999999999999999. C_m not exactly 1. p_m = C_m - v_m/2. Denom = p_m - p_1. The algorithm says C_m=1. If v sum not exactly 1, h_m may not exactly 1. We enforce h[-1]=1. But p values maybe not exactly consistent. Tolerance okay. Could normalize v to sum exactly 1? v = v / np.sum(v). But algorithm says w_i = t_i/Z, so sum should be 1. Due to rounding, bincount sum maybe 1 +/- eps. Normalizing v might improve. But if we normalize, does it alter exact expected? Tolerance. Could do v = v / np.sum(v) to ensure C_m=1. But if v sum is 0? no. This may slightly change weights. But reference likely uses same algorithm with floats? They compare tolerance. Normalizing is okay? The algorithm says w_i = t_i/Z, so v sum mathematically 1. In floating, normalizing enforces. Could be beneficial. But if v contains subnormals, sum maybe 1. Use v = v / float(np.sum(v, dtype=np.float64)). But if sum is 0? no. This adds O(m). Fine.\nHowever, if we normalize v, p_m = 1 - v_m/2 exactly? Not exactly. But h[-1] enforced.\nNeed think about exact worked examples.\nExample 1: values [1,2,3,4], lws [0,0,0,0], qs [0,0.25,0.5,0.75,1]. t all 1, Z=4, w=0.25. u [1,2,3,4], v [0.25]*4. C [0.25,0.5,0.75,1]. p [0.125,0.375,0.625,0.875]. p1=0.125, pm=0.875, denom=0.75. h [0, 1/3, 2/3, 1]. q 0.25 -> k=0, interp 1 + 0.25*(1)/(1/3)=1.75. Good.\nExample 3: values [1,1,2], w [1/3,1/3,1/3]. u [1,2], v [2/3,1/3]. C [2/3,1], p [1/3,5/6], denom=1/2, h [0,1]. q 0.5 -> 1 + 0.5*1 =1.5. Good.\nExample 4: [5,5,5,5,9], v [0.8,0.2], p [0.4,0.9], denom=0.5, h [0,1]. q .25 -> 6. Good.\nExample 5: values [0,1,2], lws [0, log2, 0]. t [0.5,1,0.5], Z=2, w [0.25,0.5,0.25]. u [0,1,2], v same. C [0.25,0.75,1], p [0.125,0.5,0.875], denom=0.75, h [0,0.5,1]. q .25 -> 0.5. Good.\nExample 6: values [10,20,30], lws [-1e6,0,-1e6]. L=0, t [0,1,0], mask2 leaves [20], m=1 return 20. Good.\n\nNeed consider validation V5: \"every log weight is -inf, or n == 0\". If n>0 and some log_weights -inf, some finite, okay. If log_weights contains -inf and NaN? V3 raises. If log_weights contains +inf? V3. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If values length != log_weights length but one is 2D? Using size may pass where len would fail. Spec 1-D. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: If qs is empty, q_arr.size=0. V4 np.any on empty false. V5 checked. Then return []. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If values/log_weights are numpy arrays with dtype object containing Python floats, np.asarray dtype float works. If they are arrays with shape (n,1), ravel size n. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If log_weights are all -inf but qs invalid, our order checks V4 before V5, so raises ValueError anyway. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If values contains inf but log_weights length mismatch, our order checks V1 first, raises. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If values is empty list, log_weights empty list, qs [0.5]. V1 pass, V2/V3/V4 pass, V5 n==0 raise. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If values empty, log_weights nonempty, V1 raise. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If log_weights empty, values nonempty, V1 raise. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If values is [1.0], log_weights [float('-inf')], qs [] -> V5 raise. Our code: n=1, V2 pass, V3 pass (isneginf not nan/+inf), V4 pass, V5 np.all(np.isneginf(lws)) true -> raise. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If log_weights [float('-inf'), float('-inf')], values [1,2], qs [0] -> V5 raise. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If log_weights [float('-inf'), 0.0], values [1,2], qs [0] -> mask leaves one, m=1 return value 2. Good.\n\nNeed think about \"Validation happens before any computation.\" Our conversion and validation before computation. Good.\n\nNeed think about \"nothing else may be raised\" for weighted_quantile. Our code may raise FloatingPointError? No. np.exp underflow may raise if np.seterr? By default underflow ignore. But if user set np.seterr(under='raise')? Could raise FloatingPointError. To be safe, use np.errstate(under='ignore', over='ignore', invalid='ignore') around exp? Since lw_i - L <=0, no overflow. Underflow possible. If global np.seterr under='raise', np.exp could raise. We can wrap with np.errstate(under='ignore', over='ignore', invalid='ignore', divide='ignore'). Good. Also np.sum no. Use with np.errstate.\nSimilarly log_sum_exp math.exp underflow? math.exp(-1000) returns 0.0? Actually math.exp(-1000) returns 0.0? It may raise OverflowError for positive, but negative underflow returns 0.0? In Python, math.exp(-1000) returns 0.0, no exception. math.exp(1000) raises OverflowError, but we subtract max so <=0. If x-m is -inf, math.exp(-i