issue: `float('12345678901234567890.123456789')` returns 1.2345678901234568e+19, absolute error ~? Not within 1e-9, but impossible. Tests likely not.\n\nCould use Decimal to compute and then float; for moderate values, float(Decimal) same. For values with more than 15 digits, float rounding. No difference.\n\nLet's implement parse_amount with float and catch.\n\nBut need be careful with separator validation for only commas: \"the comma is a grouping separator if every comma is immediately followed by exactly three digits that are themselves followed by another comma or the end of the magnitude; otherwise the comma is the decimal separator.\"\nThis condition: every comma is immediately followed by exactly three digits that are themselves followed by another comma or end. Means for each comma at index i, m[i+1:i+4] are digits, and (i+4 == len(m) or m[i+4] == ','). It doesn't require the three digits are not followed by more digits? If followed by comma or end, okay. If `1,2345` comma followed 234 then next char 5 not comma/end -> fails. Good. If `1,234,567` both commas ok. If `12,345,678` ok. If `1,234,56` second comma fails. If `1,234,567,` trailing comma? Magnitude cannot contain trailing comma? It would fail because comma at end not followed by 3 digits. If decimal comma, multiple commas invalid due decimal exactly once. Good.\nWhat about `1,234,567.89` both present, grouping comma validation not used? Both present: last dot > comma -> decimal dot grouping comma. Then validate grouping: int_part '1,234,567' groups ok. If `1,23.45` grouping comma validation would fail but both present chooses grouping comma and then validation fails. Good.\nWhat about `1.234,56` both present, last comma > dot -> decimal comma grouping dot. Validate int_part '1.234' groups ok. Good.\nWhat about `1.234.567,89` both present, last comma > dots -> decimal comma grouping dot. int_part '1.234.567' groups ok, frac '89' -> 1234567.89. Is that intended? Both present: last occurrence further right is decimal. Yes.\nWhat about `1,234.567,89` both present, last comma after dot? m: comma at 1, dot at 5, comma at 9? last comma > dot -> decimal comma, grouping dot. int_part '1,234.567' contains both comma and dot? Wait grouping is dot, but int_part also contains comma (the first comma) because decimal comma at last occurrence split: int_part = '1,234.567'. With grouping dot, groups = ['1,234', '567']; first group '1,234' not digits -> invalid. Good.\nWhat about `1.234,567.89` both present, last dot > comma -> decimal dot, grouping comma. int_part '1.234,567' contains dot and comma; grouping comma groups ['1.234','567']; first not digits invalid. Good.\n\nOnly full stops: \"full stop is a grouping separator if there are two or more of them; if exactly one, decimal.\" This means `1.234.567` grouping, `1.234` decimal. What about `1.234.56` grouping? two dots -> grouping, validate groups ['1','234','56'] invalid. Good. What about `1.23.456` grouping invalid. What about `1.234.567.890` grouping valid. What about `.123` one dot decimal int empty invalid. What about `123.` one dot decimal frac empty invalid. What about `1..234` two dots grouping, int_part '1.', groups ['1',''] invalid. Good.\n\nOnly commas: if grouping condition true, grouping; else decimal. But what if multiple commas and grouping condition false, decimal comma. Then decimal must occur exactly once. If multiple commas, invalid. Example `1,23,45`: grouping condition false (first comma followed 23 then comma? Actually first comma followed '23,' not 3 digits? fails; second followed '45' end fails) -> decimal comma, count 2 invalid. Good. `1,234,56`: grouping false -> decimal comma count 2 invalid. `1,234,567`: grouping true -> valid. `12,345`: grouping true -> valid. `123,456`: valid. `1,2345`: grouping false -> decimal comma count 1, int '1', frac '2345' -> valid? Is `1,2345` with comma decimal and fractional part 2345 allowed? Spec: decimal separator must occur exactly once, fractional part one or more digits no separators. Yes, so 1.2345. But is that intended? If only commas present and not grouping, comma is decimal separator. So `1,2345` valid 1.2345. Example invalid `\"1,23.45\"` due both. They didn't list `1,2345`. It should be valid as decimal comma with 4 fractional digits. Good.\nWhat about `1,234,567,890` grouping true valid.\nWhat about `1,234,567,89` grouping false? last comma followed 89 end fails -> decimal comma count 3 invalid. Good.\n\nNeed consider magnitude with both separators and decimal separator count exactly once. If both present and decimal is one, grouping is other. But what if decimal separator appears multiple times? Example `1.234,56.78`: both, last dot > comma -> decimal dot, grouping comma. m.count('.')=2 -> invalid. Good. `1,234.56,78`: both, last comma > dot -> decimal comma, grouping dot. m.count(',')=2 -> invalid. Good.\n\nNow date parsing details.\n\nImplement is_leap(year): year % 4 == 0 and (year % 100 != 0 or year % 400 == 0).\nmonth_lengths(year): list.\nvalid_date(y,m,d): 1000<=y<=9999, 1<=m<=12, 1<=d<=len.\n\nparse_date(field):\n    v = field.strip(' \\t')\n    if not v: return None\n    # YYYY-MM-DD\n    if len(v) == 10 and v[4]=='-' and v[7]=='-' and v[0:4].isdigit() and v[5:7].isdigit() and v[8:10].isdigit():\n         y=int(v[0:4]); m=int(v[5:7]); d=int(v[8:10]); if valid return f'{y:04d}-{m:02d}-{d:02d}' else None\n    # D/M/YYYY\n    # Need exactly 1 or 2 digits, slash, 1 or 2 digits, slash, 4 digits.\n    # Could regex: ^(\\d{1,2})/(\\d{1,2})/(\\d{4})$\n    # Use manual to avoid re? re okay.\n    if re.match(r'^\\d{1,2}/\\d{1,2}/\\d{4}$', v):\n         a,b,y = ...\n         if valid(y,b,a) return ... # day=a month=b\n         if valid(y,a,b) return ... # month=a day=b\n         return None\n    # D Mon YYYY\n    # regex ^(\\d{1,2}) +([A-Za-z]{3}) +(\\d{4})$ with month in list.\n    if re.match(r'^\\d{1,2} +[A-Za-z]{3} +\\d{4}$', v):\n         parts? Need split. Could regex groups.\n         m = re.match(r'^(\\d{1,2}) +([A-Za-z]{3}) +(\\d{4})$', v)\n         day=int(m.group(1)); mon=m.group(2).lower(); year=int(m.group(3)); if mon in month_map and valid...\n    return None\n\nNeed ensure `D Mon YYYY` spaces one or more. If multiple spaces, regex ` +` okay. If tabs, not. If field has leading/trailing tabs trimmed. Internal tabs invalid. Good.\nMonth names exactly three ASCII letters. `Jan` ok. `JAN` ok. `jan` ok. `Jan1` invalid. `Jan  2021` ok. `Jan\\t2021` invalid.\nYear exactly 4 digits. `0999` invalid due year range. `1000` valid.\nDay 1 or 2 digits. `01` valid. `00` invalid day range. `31` valid if month.\nSlash: A/B/YYYY with A/B 1 or 2 digits. `0/0/2021` invalid. `1/1/2021` valid. `13/1/2021` first day=13 month=1 valid -> Jan 13. `1/13/2021` first invalid, second month=1 day=13 valid. `13/13/2021` both invalid. `01/02/2021` first day=1 month=2 valid. `02/01/2021` first day=2 month=1 valid. Good.\nWhat about `1/2/2021` and `2/1/2021` distinct.\nWhat about `1/12/2021` first day=1 month=12 valid -> Dec 1. `12/1/2021` first day=12 month=1 valid -> Jan 12. Good.\nWhat about `12/12/2021` both valid -> first day=12 month=12.\nWhat about `02/02/2021` valid.\nWhat about `00/01/2021` first day=0 invalid, second month=0 invalid -> bad.\nWhat about `01/00/2021` bad.\nWhat about `01/13/2021` first month=13 invalid, second month=1 day=13 valid -> Jan 13. Good.\nWhat about `13/01/2021` first day=13 month=1 valid -> Jan 13.\nWhat about `13/02/2021` first day=13 month=2 valid -> Feb 13. `02/13/2021` first invalid, second month=2 day=13 valid.\nWhat about `31/02/2021` first invalid, second month=31 invalid -> bad. `02/31/2021` bad.\nLeap: `29/02/2020` first day=29 month=2 valid leap -> Feb 29. `02/29/2020` first invalid, second month=2 day=29 valid. `29/02/1900` invalid (century not 400). `29/02/2000` valid.\nYear 9999 valid, 1000 valid. `0999` invalid.\n\nNeed use re? Standard library. Could avoid re for speed but fine. Need import re. Ensure no exceptions. re.match on str okay.\n\nPotential issue: `str.isdigit()` accepts Unicode digits? We want ASCII digits 0-9. For date and amount, spec says digits. Python `isdigit` may accept superscript digits? e.g. '\u00b2'.isdigit() True? Actually '\u00b2'.isdigit() True? It is decimal? No, isdigit true for superscripts? It may. We should restrict to ASCII 0-9. Use helper `is_ascii_digit(c)` or regex `[0-9]`. For strings, use `all(c in '0123456789' for c in s)`. For date, use regex `[0-9]`. For amount magnitude, check `c in '0123456789,.'`. For groups, use `all(c in digits)`.\nUse `DIGITS = '0123456789'`.\nHelper `all_digits(s): return bool(s) and all(c in DIGITS for c in s)`.\n\nDate manual with regex using `[0-9]`.\nMonth map: {'jan':1,...}\nFor D Mon YYYY, regex `^([0-9]{1,2}) +([A-Za-z]{3}) +([0-9]{4})$`. Need ensure month letters exactly three ASCII letters. `[A-Za-z]{3}`. Then lower in map.\nCould there be non-ASCII letters? `[A-Za-z]` only ASCII. Good.\n\nField scanning details with Unicode? Spaces and tabs only ASCII? Spec says spaces and tabs. Use `c in ' \\t'`. Not other whitespace. Good.\nLine empty or contains only spaces and tabs: `line.strip(' \\t') == ''`.\nComment: find first char not space/tab. Could do `stripped = line.lstrip(' \\t')`; if not stripped ignore; if stripped[0] == '#' ignore.\nBut note: if line is `   #` ignore. If line is `#` ignore. If line is `#a,b,c` ignore.\nIf line is `a # comment` not comment, parse; unquoted field may include #.\n\nCR removal: \"If a line ends with a single '\\r', remove that character before anything else.\" Could mean if line ends with '\\r' (i.e., CRLF), remove it. If line ends with multiple '\\r', maybe only one? We'll do if line.endswith('\\r'): line = line[:-1]. If line is '\\r' -> empty ignore. If line is '\\r\\r' -> '\\r' then empty? Wait after split on '\\n', a line could be '\\r\\r' if text has '\\r\\r\\n'? It ends with '\\r', remove one -> '\\r'. Then line contains only? '\\r' is not space/tab, not empty. It would be parsed as field? But spec