_records called once, okay. But if tests call multiple times, redefines each time, fine.\nCould also use local functions inside. Need ensure no global variables? We can define constants inside.\nBut code readability? Fine.\n\nNeed only standard library. We can import re at top. Is import considered public? no.\nCould avoid re entirely to minimize imports. But re is standard. If no imports, okay. Let's consider implementing date with manual checks to avoid re. But re simpler. Need ensure no extra public function. Import re is module-level name `re`, not function. Fine.\n\nPotential hidden tests: type check. `isinstance(text, str)` raises TypeError for non-str. What about subclass of str? isinstance true, okay. If text is bytes, TypeError.\nFor any str input never raise. Need guard all helpers.\n\nLet's think through scanner edge cases thoroughly.\n\nScanner algorithm with while True. Need ensure after quoted field and comma, if line ends immediately after comma, next iteration adds empty unquoted field. Good.\nWhat about quoted field followed by comma then spaces then end: `a,\"b\",   ` -> after comma i at spaces, loop skip spaces, i==n, unquoted raw '' append. fields ['a','b','']. Good.\nWhat about quoted field followed by comma then quote: `a,\"b\",\"c\"` -> works.\nWhat about quoted field with escaped quotes at end: `\"a\"\"`? Let's parse: start quote, read a, then quote quote? If line `\"a\"\"` (4 chars? quote a quote quote). At i=2 quote, i+1 quote -> append quote, i=4, loop ends closed False -> fail. Is that correct? String intended value `a\"`? To represent `a\"` need `\"a\"\"\"` (quote a quote quote quote). `\"a\"\"` is quote a quote quote: after a, pair? Actually characters: 0 \", 1 a, 2 \", 3 \". At i=2, next is quote, so escaped quote, i=4, no closing quote -> fail. Good.\nWhat about `\"\"\"\"` (four quotes): start quote, i=1 quote, next quote -> append quote, i=3, i=3 quote, next none -> close, value `\"`. Good.\nWhat about `\"\"` empty quoted: start quote i=1, c quote, i+1 none -> close, value ''. Good.\nWhat about `\"a\" \"b\"`: after closing quote skip spaces, next char space? skip, next char quote not comma/end -> fail. Good.\nWhat about `\"a\" , \"b\"`: after closing quote skip spaces, comma ok. Next field skip spaces, quote. Good.\nWhat about unquoted field with leading/trailing spaces: ` a , b ` -> fields ['a','b'].\nWhat about unquoted field with internal spaces: `a b,c` -> field `a b`.\nWhat about unquoted field with tabs: `a\\tb,c` -> `a\\tb` (internal tab preserved). ID with internal tab? If field0 `a\\tb`, not only spaces/tabs, valid id. Date/amount trim only ends, internal tab invalid for date/amount. Note can contain anything scanner produced, including tabs, quotes? If unquoted note can contain quotes? e.g. `a,2021-01-01,1,b\"c` note `b\"c`. If quoted note, value exact.\nWhat about note with comma: must be quoted. `a,2021-01-01,1,\"b,c\"` -> 4 fields. Good.\nWhat about 4 fields with empty note: `a,2021-01-01,1,` -> fields 4, note ''. Good.\nWhat about 4 fields with quoted empty note: `a,2021-01-01,1,\"\"` -> note ''. Good.\nWhat about 5 fields: `a,b,c,d,e` -> FIELD_COUNT.\nWhat about 2 fields: `a,b` -> FIELD_COUNT.\nWhat about 1 field: `a` -> FIELD_COUNT.\nWhat about 0 fields? Empty line ignored before. Line with only comma? `,` -> fields ['','']? Let's scan: start unquoted to comma -> '', append, i comma, i++ end, loop unquoted '' append -> 2 fields. FIELD_COUNT. Good.\nLine `,,` -> 3 fields empty -> FIELD_COUNT? len 3, then BAD_ID? Wait field count 3, id empty -> BAD_ID. Spec: if number fields not 3 or 4 FIELD_COUNT. If 3, then id empty BAD_ID. So `,,` -> BAD_ID. Good.\nLine `a,,` -> 3 fields, id a, date empty BAD_DATE. Good.\nLine `a,b,` -> 3 fields, amount empty BAD_AMOUNT. Good.\nLine `a,b,c,` -> 4 fields, note empty valid. Good.\n\nStep order: scan failure BAD_QUOTE before field count. So line `a,\"b,c` -> BAD_QUOTE not FIELD_COUNT. Good.\nLine `a,\"b\"x,c` -> BAD_QUOTE.\nLine `a,\"b\" x,c` -> BAD_QUOTE.\nLine `a,\"b\" ,c` -> ok.\nLine `a,\"b\" ,` -> fields 3? a,b,'' -> amount empty BAD_AMOUNT. Good.\n\nID validation: field 0 exactly as scanner produced. If empty or only spaces/tabs. For unquoted, empty if line starts comma or spaces then comma. For quoted spaces: `\"   \",b,c` -> id '   ' -> BAD_ID. For unquoted `   ,b,c`? line starts spaces then comma: first field empty -> BAD_ID. Good.\nID with internal spaces: `a b,c,d` -> id 'a b' valid. ID with tabs: `a\\tb,c,d` valid. ID with newline? no.\nID with quote char unquoted: `\"a\",b,c`? Actually first char quote -> quoted field value a. ID 'a'. If unquoted `a\"b,c,d` -> id `a\"b` valid. Good.\n\nDate validation: field 1 may be quoted with spaces. `a,\" 2021-01-01 \",1` -> trim -> valid. `a,\"2021-01-01\",1` valid. `a,\"2021-1-1\",1` invalid (not 2 digits). `a,\"2021-01-1\",1` invalid. `a,\"2021-01-01 \",1` valid. `a,\" 2021-01-01\",1` valid.\nDate with plus: `+2021-01-01` invalid.\nDate with empty: invalid.\nDate with `2021-01-01` year 2021 valid. `0999-01-01` invalid year. `1000-01-01` valid. `9999-12-31` valid.\nDate with `2021-02-29` invalid non-leap. `2020-02-29` valid. `1900-02-29` invalid. `2000-02-29` valid.\nDate with `2021-13-01` invalid. `2021-00-01` invalid. `2021-01-00` invalid. `2021-01-32` invalid.\nSlash: `2021-01-01` not slash. `1/1/2021` valid. `01/01/2021` valid. `1/01/2021` valid. `01/1/2021` valid. `1/1/0999` invalid year. `1/1/1000` valid.\n`1/1/20211` invalid (year 5 digits). `1/1/202` invalid.\n`12/31/2021` first day=12 month=31 invalid, second month=12 day=31 valid -> Dec 31. `31/12/2021` first day=31 month=12 valid -> Dec 31.\n`31/1/2021` first day=31 month=1 valid -> Jan 31. `1/31/2021` first invalid, second month=1 day=31 valid -> Jan 31.\n`30/2/2021` invalid both. `2/30/2021` invalid.\n`31/4/2021` first day=31 month=4 invalid, second month=31 invalid -> bad. `4/31/2021` first day=4 month=31 invalid, second month=4 day=31 invalid -> bad.\n`1/2/2021` valid Feb 1. `2/1/2021` valid Jan 2.\nMonth name: `1 Jan 2021`, `01 Jan 2021`, `1 jan 2021`, `1 JAN 2021`, `1  Jan  2021` valid. `1 Jan  2021` valid. `1 Jan 2021 ` trim. `1 Jan 0999` invalid. `1 Jan 1000` valid. `31 Jan 2021` valid. `32 Jan 2021` invalid. `1 Feb 2021` valid. `29 Feb 2020` valid. `29 Feb 1900` invalid. `1 March 2021` invalid (month name 5 letters). `1 Janu 2021` invalid. `1 Jan 20211` invalid. `1 Jan 202` invalid. `1 Jan 2021` with tab between? `1\\tJan\\t2021` invalid because regex spaces only. But maybe should accept tabs? Let's examine spec: \"D Mon YYYY: 1 or 2 digit day, one or more spaces, exactly three ASCII letters naming the month ..., one or more spaces, exactly 4 digits.\" It says spaces, not tabs. In other places they say spaces and tabs for trimming. So strict spaces. Good.\nWhat about non-breaking space? invalid.\n\nAmount edge cases more.\n\nLet's formalize parse_amount with helper all_digits.\n\nPseudo:\ndef parse_amount(raw):\n    s = raw.strip(' \\t')\n    if not s: return None\n    negative = False\n    if len(s) >= 2 and s[0] == '(' and s[-1] == ')':\n        negative = True\n        s = s[1:-1].strip(' \\t')\n        if not s: return None\n        if s[0] in '+-': return None\n    else:\n        if s[0] == '(' or s[-1] == ')': return None\n    sign = 1\n    if s[0] in '+-':\n        if s[0] == '-': sign = -1\n        s = s[1:]\n    if s.startswith('$'):\n        s = s[1:]\n    percent = False\n    if s.endswith('%'):\n        percent = True\n        s = s[:-1]\n    m = s\n    if not m or any(c not in '0123456789,.' for c in m): return None\n    has_comma = ',' in m\n    has_dot = '.' in m\n    decimal = grouping = None\n    if has_comma and has_dot:\n        if m.rfind(',') > m.rfind('.'):\n            decimal, grouping = ',', '.'\n        else:\n            decimal, grouping = '.', ','\n    elif has_comma:\n        if _commas_grouping(m):\n            grouping = ','\n        else:\n            decimal = ','\n    elif has_dot:\n        if m.count('.') >= 2:\n            grouping = '.'\n        else:\n            decimal = '.'\n    # validate\n    if decimal is not None:\n        if m.count(decimal) != 1: return None\n        int_part, frac = m.split(decimal, 1)\n        if not all_digits(frac): return None\n    else:\n        int_part = m\n        frac = ''\n    if grouping is not None:\n        if int_part.count(grouping) < 1: return None\n        groups = int_part.split(grouping)\n        if len(groups) < 2: return None\n        if not (1 <= len(groups[0]) <= 3) or not all_digits(groups[0]): return None\n        for g in groups[1:]:\n            if len(g) != 3 or not all_digits(g): return None\n    else:\n        if not all_digits(int_part): return None\n    # compute\n    int_digits = int_part.replace(grouping, '') if grouping else int_part\n    if not all_digits(int_digits): return None\n    try:\n        if frac:\n            value = float(int_digits + '.' + frac)\n        else:\n            value = float(int_digits)\n    except (OverflowError, ValueError):\n        # determine sign\n        if negative or sign == -1:\n            return float('-inf')\n        return float('inf')\n    if percent:\n        value /= 100.0\n    if negative or sign == -1:\n        value = -value\n    return value\n\nNeed check `s[0]` after paren removal: if s empty, return None before s[0]. Good.\nAfter sign removal, s could be empty. Then startswith('$') false, endswith('%') false, m empty invalid. Good.\nAfter $ removal, s could be empty. invalid.\nAfter % removal, s could be empty. invalid.\nWhat about `+` alone: sign removed, m empty invalid.\nWhat about `$` alone: m empty invalid.\nWhat about `%` alone: percent removed, m empty invalid.\nWhat about `+$%`? $ removed, % removed, m empty invalid.\nWhat about `+12%` valid.\nWhat about `+12.5%` valid.\nWhat about `+1,234.50%` valid 12.345? 1234.5/100=12.345.\nWhat about `+1.234.567%` valid 12345.67.\n\nPotential issue: `float(int_digits + '.' + frac)` for int_digits huge and frac huge may be slow but okay. If int_digits is ''? validated nonempty. If frac nonempty.\nIf value is `-0.0`? For negative zero? e.g. `-0` -> value 0.0, negative -> -0.0. Python float -0.0 equals 0.0. Spec doesn't care. `(-0)` -> -0.0. Good.\nIf percent and negative zero? okay.\n\nBut need ensure \"correct to within 1e-9 of mathematical value\". Using float direct is nearest binary, error < 0.5 ulp. For values around 1, error ~1e-16. For values around 1e9, ulp ~1e-7, within 1e-9? No, absolute error could be >1e-9. But impossible to represent all within 1e-9 absolute for large floats. They likely mean relative? Or test small. Could use Decimal to compute exact then float, same. No way around float. Maybe they expect float.\n\nCould return `float` from Decimal with high precision? Same final float.\n\nNow duplicate ids and errors. Need ensure if line fails after some fields, no record. Good.\n\nLet's consider line with BAD_QUOTE and duplicate id? no record.\n\nNow possible hidden tests around field scanning and quotes.\n\nLet's manually test scanner with examples:\n`a,\"b,c\",d` -> fields:\ni0 unquoted a, comma, i2? line: a , \" b , c \" , d. After a comma i=2? Actually index: 0 a,1 comma,2 quote. loop skip none, quote, read b, comma? Wait inside quoted, comma is literal, read c, quote close, skip none, next comma, append b,c, i after comma, unquoted d. fields ['a','b,c','d'].\n`x,\"say \"\"hi\"\"\" , 1`:\nline: x , \" s a y space \" \" h i \" \" \" space , space 1.\nFirst x. Second quoted: read say space, pair -> quote, hi, pair? Let's index triple: after hi there are three quotes. At first of triple, next is quote -> append quote, skip two, now at third quote. Next char space, so close. Value say \"hi\". skip spaces, comma. Third unquoted ` 1` trim 1. Good.\n`a,\"b\"x,c`: after close quote, skip none, next x -> fail.\n`a,\"b,c`: no close -> fail.\n\nWhat about `a,\"b\"\"c\",d`: value b\"c. Good.\nWhat about `a,\"b\"\"\",d`: characters quote b quote quote quote? At b, then triple? Actually `\"b\"\"\"` = quote b quote quote quote. At first quote after b, next quote -> append quote, i at third quote, next char comma? close. Value b\". Good.\nWhat about `a,\"b\"\"\",d` intended b\"? yes.\nWhat about `a,\"b\"\"\",d` with four quotes? `\"b\"\"\"\"`? value b\"? Let's not.\n\nNeed ensure scanner doesn't treat `\"\"` after closing quote as escaped? It only escapes inside before closing. After closing, next char must comma/end. So `\"a\"\"` fails as above. Good.\n\nNow line ignoring: Step 2 before comment. If line empty or only spaces/tabs ignore. If line `   ` ignore. If line `\\t` ignore. If line `  #` comment ignore. If line `#` ignore. If line `#a,b,c` ignore. If line `a #` not ignore.\n\nNow type check: `if not isinstance(text, str): raise TypeError`. Should we raise TypeError with message? okay.\n\nNow final code structure.\n\nOption 1: top-level imports and nested helpers.\n\n```\nimport re\n\ndef parse_records(text):\n    if not isinstance(text, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"text must be str\")\n    DIGITS = '0123456789'\n    MONTHS = {...}\n    def all_digits(s): ...\n    def is_leap(y): ...\n    def valid_date(y,m,d): ...\n    def normalize(y,m,d): ...\n    def parse_date(v): ...\n    def commas_grouping(m): ...\n    def parse_amount(raw): ...\n    def scan_fields(line): ...\n    records=[]; errors=[]; index={}\n    for lineno, line in enumerate(text.split('\\n'), 1):\n        if line.endswith('\\r'):\n            line = line[:-1]\n        if line.strip(' \\t') == '':\n            continue\n        stripped = line.lstrip(' \\t')\n        if stripped[0] == '#':\n            continue\n        fields = scan_fields(line)\n        if fields is None:\n            errors.append((lineno, 'BAD_QUOTE')); continue\n        if len(fields) not in (3,4):\n            errors.append((lineno, 'FIELD_COUNT')); continue\n        rid = fields[0]\n        if rid.strip(' \\t') == '':\n            errors.append((lineno, 'BAD_ID')); continue\n        date = parse_date(fields[1])\n        if date is None:\n            errors.append((lineno, 'BAD_DATE')); continue\n        amount = parse_amount(fields[2])\n        if amount is None:\n            errors.append((lineno, 'BAD_AMOUNT')); continue\n        note = fields[3] if len(fields) == 4 else ''\n        rec = {'id': rid, 'date': date, 'amount': amount, 'note': note}\n        if rid in index:\n            records[index[rid]] = rec\n        else:\n            index[rid] = len(records)\n            records.append(rec)\n    return records, errors\n```\n\nNeed ensure `stripped` not empty after line.strip check. If line only spaces/tabs, continue. So stripped[0] safe.\n\nPotential issue: `line.strip(' \\t')` removes both ends. For line with only spaces/tabs, empty. Good.\n`line.lstrip(' \\t')` for comment. Good.\n\nNow parse_date implementation with re. Need compile regex? Could define inside. Use `re.match` each call. Fine. But if we import re, okay. Could avoid re by manual to reduce dependency. Let's consider manual date to avoid regex and ensure ASCII.\n\nManual YYYY-MM-DD:\n```\nif len(v)==10 and v[4]=='-' and v[7]=='-' and all_digits(v[0:4]) and all_digits(v[5:7]) and all_digits(v[8:10]):\n   ...\n```\nManual D/M/YYYY:\nNeed find slashes. Could check length? A/B/YYYY with A/B 1-2 digits, year 4. Total length 7 to 9? Actually 1+1+1+1+4=7, 2+1+1+1+4=8, 1+1+2+1+4=8, 2+1+2+1+4=9. Could regex easier.\nManual:\n```\nparts = v.split('/')\nif len(parts)==3 and all_digits(parts[0]) and 1<=len(parts[0])<=2 and all_digits(parts[1]) and 1<=len(parts[1])<=2 and all_digits(parts[2]) and len(parts[2])==4:\n   ...\n```\nThis is simple and avoids re. But need ensure no extra slashes. split('/') len 3. Good. If part empty, all_digits false. If part has non-digit, false. Good.\nManual D Mon YYYY:\nNeed split by spaces? But one or more spaces. Could use regex or manual. We can use `re` for this one. Or manual: find first space, last space? Month exactly three letters. Could do:\n```\n#