ecord, adjust collects unchanged. Good.\n\nOkay, enough.\n\nNeed think about exact exception for unknown kind. Legacy:\nelse:\n    raise ValueError('unknown kind: %r' % (kind,))\nIf kind is a custom object whose __repr__ raises, legacy raises that exception? Actually formatting %r calls repr, if raises, that exception propagates instead of ValueError. Our code: `raise ValueError('unknown kind: %r' % (kind,))` also calls repr before raising. If repr raises, that exception propagates. Good. If we use `from None`, and repr raises, the exception is not ValueError. Same.\n\nIf kind is unhashable, legacy doesn't call repr until raising ValueError. It does call repr in message. Our code catches TypeError from lookup, then constructs message with repr. If repr raises, that exception propagates. Legacy would also construct message and if repr raises, propagate. Good.\n\nIf kind is a custom object whose __repr__ has side effects, both call once. Good.\n\nIf kind is a custom object whose __repr__ raises, both raise that exception, not ValueError. Is that \"unknown kind\" behaviour? Legacy would. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If kind is unhashable and its __repr__ raises, our code catches TypeError from lookup, then tries to format, raises repr exception. Legacy would format and raise repr exception. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If kind is unhashable and its __repr__ has side effects, both call once. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If kind is unhashable and its __repr__ returns a string, our code raises ValueError. Legacy raises ValueError. Good.\n\nPotential issue: If kind is unhashable and its __hash__ has side effects, our code calls __hash__ during lookup, legacy does not. This is a difference. If kind is an unhashable object, __hash__ may be None or raise. If it's a custom object with __hash__ that has side effects but is unhashable? Unhashable means __hash__ is None or raises TypeError. If __hash__ has side effects before raising, our code triggers, legacy doesn't. Is that observable?